package cajeta.io.net.uri;

import cajeta.io.net.NetException;
import cajeta.lang.String;

/**
 * Thrown by [`Uri.parse`](Uri.cajeta) when the input is not a
 * well-formed URI per **RFC 3986**. The `position` field carries the
 * 0-based byte offset at which the parser noticed the problem, so the
 * caller can point at the offending character.
 *
 * ## Hierarchy
 *
 * The networking spec ([`docs/Net.md`](../../../../../docs/Net.md)
 * §Error model) places `MalformedUri` under
 * [`cajeta.io.net.NetException`](../NetException.cajeta) (plan item
 * **NET-6.5**), the networking peer of `cajeta.io.file.IoException`.
 * `NetException` (plan item **NET-1.8**) has now landed, so this class
 * extends it directly — every `cajeta.io.net` failure, socket-layer or
 * URI-layer, is catchable as a single `NetException`. (NET-6.1
 * originally rooted this on `cajeta.error.RecoverableException` as a
 * placeholder, because NET-1.8 had not yet been built; NET-6.5 is that
 * one-line reparent.)
 *
 * `NetException extends cajeta.error.RecoverableException`, so the
 * `Recoverable` discipline is unchanged: a caller of `Uri.parse`
 * either catches `MalformedUriException` (or the `NetException` root)
 * explicitly, or declares it in its `throws` clause and propagates
 * upward — exactly as the [`cajeta.io.file`](../../io/file/IoException.cajeta)
 * hierarchy does. The `(message, position)` constructor contract is
 * **unchanged** from NET-6.1; existing call sites and
 * `e.position`-reading catch blocks keep working byte-for-byte.
 *
 * ## The inherited `kind`
 *
 * As a `NetException`, every instance carries the cross-platform
 * [`kind`](../NetException.cajeta) ordinal so a single
 * `catch (NetException e)` can branch on `e.kind`. A malformed URI is
 * a *parse* failure, not an errno-derived socket error, so it is
 * classified to [`NetException.KIND_INVALID`](../NetException.cajeta)
 * (12) — the "invalid input" bucket (the
 * [`Errors.md`](../../../../../docs/specification/io/net/Errors.md) chart
 * maps `EINVAL` and friends here). The precise byte offset still lives
 * in `position`; `kind` is the coarse cross-subtype discriminant.
 *
 * This mirrors [`cajeta.codec.json.JsonParseException`](../../codec/json/JsonParseException.cajeta),
 * which carries the same `(message, position)` shape.
 */
public class MalformedUriException extends NetException {
    /**
     * Byte offset (0-based) into the original input at which the
     * parser rejected the URI. `-1` when the failure is not tied to a
     * single position (e.g. a wholly empty input).
     */
    public int64 position;

    public MalformedUriException(#String message, int64 position) {
        this.message = message;
        this.cause = 0;
        this.kind = 12;   // NetException.KIND_INVALID
        this.position = position;
    }
}
